I see a potential issue in `getlength`:

```c
is_onebyte_result = (c[*p] & 0x80) == 0;
digits = c[(*p)++] & 0x7f;
if (*p + digits >= l) {
        return DER_BAD;
}
```

Wait, `*p + digits >= l` - this checks if `*p + digits` is >= `l`. But then it reads `digits` bytes starting at `*p`. If `*p + digits == l`, then reading `digits` bytes from `*p` would read bytes at positions `*p` through `*p + digits - 1 = l - 1`, which is valid. But the check `*p + digits >= l` would reject this case. So this is overly conservative, not an OOB.

Actually wait, let me re-read. `*p + digits >= l` means if `*p + digits == l`, it returns DER_BAD. But reading `digits` bytes from `*p` would access positions `*p` through `*p + digits - 1`. If `*p + digits == l`, then the last byte accessed is `l - 1`, which is valid. So this check is overly conservative but not an OOB.

Hmm, but what about `gettag`:

```c
static uint32_t
gettag(const uint8_t *c, size_t *p, size_t l)
{
        uint32_t tag;

        if (*p >= l)
                return DER_BAD;

        tag = c[(*p)++] & 0x1f;

        if (tag != 0x1f)
                return tag;

        if (*p >= l)
                return DER_BAD;

        while (c[*p] >= 0x80) {
                tag = tag * 128 + c[(*p)++] - 0x80;
                if (*p >= l)
                        return DER_BAD;
        }
        return tag;
}
```

In the while loop, `c[*p]` is read first, then `*p` is incremented. If `*p == l - 1` and `c[*p] >= 0x80`, then `c[*p]` is valid (reads the last byte), then `*p` becomes `l`. Then the check `*p >= l` returns DER_BAD. So no OOB.

